        <tr>
          <td><span class="postbody"></span><table width="90%" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="3" border="0" align="center"><tr> 	  <td><span class="genmed"><b>JonahD wrote:</b></span></td>	</tr>	<tr>	  <td class="quote">
<br />
That's not entirely true.  It is true that for the moment developers can (and do) charge whatever they like for their service without worrying about customer complaints, since the gamers will just pay it and be done with it.  However, the reasoning behind the cellphone companies' billing scheme is that the customer is basically paying for the use of a resource.  A cellphone network has a limited amount of resources that can be used to process calls, and when they're gone, nobody else can make calls until they're freed up.  So obviously the company has to make money for the time that the resources are being used.  So when you buy a cellphone plan you're paying for X number of minutes in advance, but the company is hoping that you'll stay within those X minutes, because if you're not using your phone, then somebody else can be using theirs.  The fees for going over your minutes are basically just a deterrent to keep you within your alloted minutes.  A cell phone company would <span style="font-weight: bold">always</span> rather you switch to a higher (and more expensive) plan, rather than go over your minutes, because that's guaranteed money, as opposed to overages which are going to fluctuate.  That's also the reason why you get so many more night and weekend minutes with typical plans.  Those periods as a rule are very underutilized, so the company would like to get more people using their phones in those time periods, to maximize resource usage.</td>	</tr></table><span class="postbody">
<br />

<br />
And my quote isn't true?  I worked directly for sales in cell phones, while the plans are built towards you setting up a plan seeing as how thats how they get their money, they make more money from people going "over" their limits than they do from actually plans.  They'll take you to court or even garnish your wages to get that extra money from you.  Because in the end thats their bread and butter.  Even when trained to cell these things many are told to undersell the plans...make it seem like 100 minutes is 1 billion.  So that people feel confortable with that amount.  Then when you go over and decide not to pay for the extra minutes you  built up, you cancel your plan and not only owe for the extra minutes, but now you pay for the termination fee.  Society today is so "pro" cell phone now, they think that they can't live without it.  Which is why every tom, dick and harry MUST have one.  I do agree with the whole weekend minutes and trying to get people to optimize the network, but all in all its about making money.
<br />

<br />
Mind you the company that I worked for might not be as customer oriented as the company you work for, I know sprint was very good at its engineers believing they were working for the good of people and such and essentially pulling the sheet over our eyes.  I only know this because of the exposure I got from the sales end of their stuff after I was no longer an engineer.  Cingular, T-Mobile and Sprint, all the same deal as far as I'm concerned.
<br />

<br />

<br />
</span><table width="90%" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="3" border="0" align="center"><tr> 	  <td><span class="genmed"><b>JonahD wrote:</b></span></td>	</tr>	<tr>	  <td class="quote">The same concept could apply to MMORPGs, albeit to a lesser extent.  The resources in this case would be bandwidth, and the same rules apply.  If you're not using bandwidth to play, then somebody else could be using it.  People with lower plans would tend to try and stick within their plan, thus freeing up resources on the off hours for others who want to play.  The real difference, I think, is that MMORPG customers seem to be willing to put up with a lot more crap than cell phone customers.  Take a look at lag, disconnects, bugs, etc.. None of that crap would be tolerated by the average cellphone customer.  Plus, you've got people willing to pay the same price as somebody else for something that they can use 10% as much.  If I had to pay the same price for 50 cellphone minutes as another customer pays for 500, I'd be finding another carrier.  Gamers don't seem to be willing to do that.  Would it eat into the bottom line of the gaming companies?  Probably, but come on.  I haven't seen any numbers, but I'd be willing to bet that the profit margin on a successful MMORPG is astronomical, otherwise there wouldn't be so many coming out.</td>	</tr></table><span class="postbody">
<br />

<br />
I don't disagree that this is a good idea for a casual gamer.  Playing games and using a cell phone are way too different for this to work the same, as you have pointed out.  If you look at it from when cell phones first came out though, people did put up with lag, disconnects and bugs.  Why, is the most common question to this...and the answer is, socially acceptable, or its a MUST have fora  business man.  Even though it doesn't work as intended.  Sprint STILL has issues with bad reception and dropping calls.  With that being a problem they still sign hundreds of people up a week in kc alone, and thats just the few best buys in the area....thats not counting the sprint stores.<br />_________________<br />[57 Luminary] Brrakk Attackk (Ogre) &lt;Guildless&gt; Zone:Sebilis &lt;7th Hammer&gt;
<br />

<br />
[56 Myrmidon] Leitbur De'Jinn (Dark Elf) &lt;Guildless&gt; Zone:Sebilis &lt;7th Hammer&gt;</span><span class="gensmall"><i></i></span></td>
