          <td><span class="postbody">Well, you also have to look at it this way.. regardless of the casual gamer discussion..
<br />

<br />
MMORPGs are really not for the casual gamer.  Why would you pay a montly fee for 6+ hours a week of gaming?  You'll never keep up with friends that do play more than that, or get to the "big stuff" to get your money's worth in the long term.  I'm not saying it wouldn't be worth it, maybe it would be, but I don't consider myself a casual EQ player and I play maybe 10-20 hours a week depending on how often we log on to raid.
<br />

<br />
As for Jonah's idea of an hourly rate, that may satisfy casual gamers, but hourly is always more expensive in the long run, and if you find you were playing excessively, you'd just switch to the montly fee anyhow.
<br />

<br />
I'm not going to sign up for an online game if I don't have the time for it.  There were periods where we didn't have time for anything, and we stopped playing..
<br />

<br />
I don't really care how Blizzard wants to solve the satisfaction level of WoW, because I know that the 24/7 players are always going to get the best benefits.. I can live with that, because I have never been that type of player.  When I play these games, I am more about the exploration and encounters and game design than anything else.    If that means I have to spend an hour travelling (we all did when EQ opened) then so be it.
<br />

<br />
The main problem with the new breed of MMORPGs is that people are spoiled.  I can TP in EQ, why can't I in WoW?  This game sucks!  I won't play it.  People want instant satisfaction.. well, that's not what these games are about, but see, the companies want to make money and they have to give in to the players' demands.  The players want to be level 100 right NOW.  I don't.. Look at my EQ characters.. my main character, a warrior, for many years.. is still level 55 after 5 years of playing!  I have more /played time on him than any character, even my "main" cleric and I enjoyed his progression.  Now, people are spoiled though.. who wants to spend years building a character (although people always did in DnD..), times have changed.
<br />

<br />
You see how long it took people to max out their class in SWG.. what, 3 months?  That wouldn't make the monthly price tag worth it,and really it isn't in the company's best interest money-wise to make progression so quick.. but as I said, people are spoiled and won't play if they can't be a Jedi in 3 months...
<br />

<br />
It's a tough thing to balance.. but as the saying goes, you can't please everyone.. I would rather they (any game developer) focus on making the time spent FUN and not worrying about all this other bullshit.. But, they are businesses, and potentially high money-makers, so we get stuck with the bullshit anyhow.<br />_________________<br />- Me</span><span class="gensmall"><i></i></span></td>
        </tr>
